WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.704
Every hub method argument is untrusted
input.

2
00:00:03.784 --> 00:00:09.452
The fact that a message arrives through
SignalR instead of an HTTP controller does

3
00:00:09.532 --> 00:00:10.764
not make it safer.

4
00:00:10.844 --> 00:00:15.362
Required fields, types, lengths, ranges,
formats, enum values,

5
00:00:15.442 --> 00:00:20.453
and business rules should be validated
before the server changes state,

6
00:00:20.533 --> 00:00:25.216
queries sensitive data, joins a group, or
sends a targeted message.

7
00:00:25.296 --> 00:00:27.514
Narrow message contracts help.

8
00:00:27.594 --> 00:00:33.098
Use DTOs that describe the specific
operation rather than binding broad domain

9
00:00:33.178 --> 00:00:35.478
objects directly to hub methods.

10
00:00:35.558 --> 00:00:41.308
A narrow contract makes it clearer which
values the client is allowed to provide and

11
00:00:41.388 --> 00:00:45.167
which values must come from trusted
server-side data.

12
00:00:45.247 --> 00:00:50.669
It also limits accidental exposure when
domain objects gain new fields later.

13
00:00:50.749 --> 00:00:54.856
Pay close attention to identifiers
supplied by the client.

14
00:00:54.936 --> 00:01:00.276
User names, tenant IDs, group names,
target IDs, room IDs, conversation IDs,

15
00:01:00.356 --> 00:01:04.545
and message IDs can all influence routing
or authorization.

16
00:01:04.625 --> 00:01:10.129
The server should verify those values
against trusted data and current policy.

17
00:01:10.209 --> 00:01:15.631
A client-supplied tenant or target should
not become the authority for where a

18
00:01:15.711 --> 00:01:16.697
message goes.

19
00:01:16.777 --> 00:01:19.323
Content also has a display boundary.

20
00:01:19.403 --> 00:01:25.071
User-generated text or markup sent through
SignalR may later appear in a browser,

21
00:01:25.151 --> 00:01:28.273
desktop view, notification surface, or
log.

22
00:01:28.353 --> 00:01:31.310
Safe rendering, encoding, sanitization,

23
00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:36.894
and content rules should be selected based
on where the message is displayed and

24
00:01:36.974 --> 00:01:39.439
what the receiving client expects.

25
00:01:39.519 --> 00:01:45.433
Serialization settings and protocol
choices should be reviewed for compatibility,

26
00:01:45.513 --> 00:01:47.238
size, and data exposure.

27
00:01:47.318 --> 00:01:52.165
The goal is a predictable contract: inputs
are intentionally shaped,

28
00:01:52.245 --> 00:01:54.956
invalid messages are rejected safely,

29
00:01:55.036 --> 00:01:58.650
sensitive fields are not accidentally
serialized,

30
00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:04.645
and authorization depends on trusted
server decisions rather than client-provided

31
00:02:04.725 --> 00:02:05.300
claims.
